* Bubble cloud, part 3: the nonlinear cloud against an independent integrator.
*
* The 199-cell cloud, damped harder (mu = 0.05, as in the paper's Fig. 16 run) so that it forgets
* its start and an integrator's error does not grow. It is driven at omega = 0.25, just below the
* first cloud resonance, at g = 0.1 for 30 periods, and every cell is sampled once per period.
* The radii swing from 0.84 to 1.61, so the nonlinear part of (A1) is fully at work.
* Reference: bubble_plots.py integrates the same 398 equations with scipy (DOP853, rtol 1e-10),
* solving (A1) for the pressure at every step with a banded solve, as the paper's (A3) does.
* What to look for (measured at 2.44.0+7, --reltol 1e-5): every radius at every sample within
*   4.6e-4 of scipy's. A 1 % error planted in one term of (A1) moves the radii by 0.62, so the
*   check tells a wrong equation from integration error. At mu = 0.002 (the sweeps' value) the
*   difference grows as ~e^(0.024 t), the same rate as between scipy's own two tolerances: that
*   cloud amplifies any small difference, and pointwise checks are not possible there.
* Plot and check: python docs/examples/multiphysics/bubble_plots.py target/release/va-cli
* Run: va-cli sim circuits/multiphysics/bubble_cloud_check.net --model models/multiphysics/bubble --tran --reltol 1e-5 --report pb
XD pb bubble_drive g=0.1 gdot=0 omega=0.25 nper=50
